00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.290
GABRIELA FERRARA: Hello.

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:03.570
In this video, we are
going to do an introduction

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.600
on how Google optimizes
storage on persistent disks

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.890
to increase database performance
and why we can disable safely

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.785
the double write
feature in MySQL

00:00:13.785 --> 00:00:17.040
even though everybody
recommends not doing it.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.810
Having that in mind, we
are proud to announce

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.270
that in the past 12 months,
the performance of Cloud

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:33.350
SQL for MySQL and
[INAUDIBLE] has doubled.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.000
According to the
benchmarks, when

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.800
moving from 128 to 256
threads, show the performance

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.750
a change of 1,502 tps
through 3,002 tps.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.200
Not bad.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.440
It's not enough to
show performance.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.200
More importantly, it's to
show consistent performance.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.230
One of the keys to get
this type of performance

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:57.110
for your own deployments
realizes setting some custom

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:59.130
flags or system variables.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:02.150
For instance, I know
the B log file size.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:06.350
I know the BIO capacity
and max allowed packet.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:10.190
To understand what each of
those flags and system variables

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:15.350
does, I recommend you
to check out this URL.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:16.940
The results are incredible.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.890
All this scattered
performance points

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.180
seem to now converge
to a better alignment.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:26.150
And when you have your
configuration set just right,

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:30.410
you get results like this where
scatter performance converges

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:32.450
to a more consistent alignment.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.570
The main factors driving this
changing performance lining

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:39.560
disabling double writes
in the InnoDB engine.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:40.920
| know what you're thinking.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.220
Why are you doing
something that everybody

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.320
recommends you not to?

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:49.430
Why is Google taking
such a risk with my data?

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.680
The secret has to do
with understanding

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.540
how block sizes influences
performance at each layer.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:59.240
On the first layer, we have
the persistent disk car

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:03.430
allowing 16 kilobytes as
physical block for writes.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:08.330
On the second layer, we
have the ext4 file system

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:11.180
using a feature called
Big Lock that allows

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.540
for 16 kilobyte cluster sizes.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.090
This is why the 16
kilobyte instance

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:20.060
allows us to disabled
double write safely.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:23.570
The third layer involves
a container-optimized OS.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:26.660
You may have heard
about it in Kubernetes.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:29.210
And at last, we
have MySQL InnoDB

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.600
configured to have
double write off

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.170
and to use the old
direct as a flush method.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.300
In a simplified way,
these four layers

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.900
are the key components of
the 16 kilobyte instance.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.060
Our engineers work
in collaboration

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.890
with multiple teams
to make this happen.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.830
If you want to know
more details as how

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:52.510
we did it with confidence,
watch the talk.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:56.030
Oh, and by the way, you don't
have to take my word for it.

00:02:56.030 --> 00:03:01.280
Our benchmarking code is open
sourced as PerfKitBenchmarker.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.200
It can be found that
GitHub, and we'll

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.170
let you compare performance
for various products

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:09.500
across several call providers.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:12.800
Check out the full talk for
more in the trenches details

